==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pewǵ-}}
{{bor+|en|la-med|pūnctuātus}}, {{lg|perfect}} {{lg|passive}} {{lg|participle}} of {{m|la|pūnctuō||to mark with points}}, from {{der|en|la-med|pūnctus||a point, dot}}, from {{m|la|pungō||to prick, punch}}; see {{af|en|-ate|id1=verb|pos1=verb-forming suffix}} and {{af|en|-ate|id1=adjective|pos1=adjective-forming suffix}}. {{dbt|en|punch}}.

===Pronunciation===
** {{IPA|en|/ˈpʰʌŋktjuːeɪt/|a=RP}}
*** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-punctuate.wav|a=Southern England}}
** {{IPA|en|[ˈpʰʌŋkʃɪu̯ːeɪt]|[ˈpʰʌŋkʃɪu̯ːeɪʔ]|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Zazollo-punctuate.wav|a=United States}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To add [[punctuation]] to. {{defdate|from 1675}}
#: {{ux|en|That occurrence of "its" needs to be '''punctuated''' as "it's".}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[add]] or to [[interrupt]] at regular intervals. {{defdate|from 1848}}
#: {{ux|en|My father '''punctuated''' his tirade with thumps on the desk.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian
|date=October 15 2020
|url=https://www.theguardian.com/news/2020/oct/15/dangerous-rise-of-military-ai-drone-swarm-autonomous-weapons
|author=Frank Pasquale
|title=‘Machines set loose to slaughter’: the dangerous rise of military AI
|passage=Most soldiers would testify that the everyday experience of war is long stretches of boredom '''punctuated''' by sudden, terrifying spells of disorder.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013065806/https://www.theguardian.com/news/2020/oct/15/dangerous-rise-of-military-ai-drone-swarm-autonomous-weapons|archivedate=13 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[emphasize]]; to [[stress]]. {{defdate|from 1883}}

